window.MOD={"n": 3, "title": "Joints: Structure, Range and Stability", "hours": 2.5, "tagline": "Every pose is a set of joint actions, and knowing how joints are built, what limits them and what holds them steady is how you teach real bodies instead of ideal shapes.", "overview": "A joint (also called an articulation) is any place where two or more bones meet. Some joints barely move at all, like the sutures of the skull, while others, like the hip and shoulder, move in every direction. In this module you will learn the three structural classes of joint, the six types of synovial joint and the movements each one allows, and the vocabulary you need to describe any pose as a list of joint actions.\n\nYou will also meet two ideas that change how you see a class. First, range of motion ends either by tension (tissue reaching its stretch limit) or by compression (something running into something else), and bone shape differs from person to person, so two students may never reach the same shape. Second, joints are held stable passively by bone fit, capsule and ligaments, and actively by muscle. That is why very flexible students need strength and control, and why stiff students need patience, props and respect rather than force. Teachers adapt and refer; they never diagnose.", "objectives": ["Classify a joint as fibrous, cartilaginous or synovial and give a body example of each.", "Name the six synovial joint types, give an example of each, and state the movements each allows.", "Describe any yoga pose as a list of joint actions using flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation and related terms.", "Distinguish tension from compression as the end of a range of motion and explain how individual bone shape limits a pose.", "Explain passive and active joint stability and compare the hip with the shoulder.", "Recognise hypermobile and hypomobile patterns in class and choose safe cues and props without diagnosing."], "lessons": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial joints", "mins": 20, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Where two bones meet", "points": ["A joint, or articulation, is any place bones meet", "Structure asks: what tissue holds the bones together?", "Function asks: how much movement does the joint allow?", "Three structural classes: fibrous, cartilaginous, synovial"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-joint-classes", "caption": "The three structural classes, from most stable to most mobile"}, "notes": "Let's start with a simple definition. A joint, which anatomists also call an articulation, is anywhere two or more bones meet. There are two ways to sort joints. The structural way asks what connects the bones: dense fibrous tissue, cartilage, or a fluid-filled capsule. The functional way asks how much the joint moves: not at all, a little, or freely. The two systems line up quite well. Fibrous joints mostly do not move, cartilaginous joints move a little, and synovial joints move freely. Notice the arrow along the bottom of the diagram. As we move from left to right we gain mobility and give up some built-in stability. Keep that trade-off in mind, because it comes back in every lesson of this module, especially when we compare the hip and the shoulder."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "The three structural classes", "columns": [{"head": "Fibrous", "points": ["Bones joined by dense fibrous tissue", "No cavity, little or no movement", "Skull sutures, tooth in socket", "Tibia and fibula held by ligament"]}, {"head": "Cartilaginous", "points": ["Bones joined by cartilage", "No cavity, slight movement", "Intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis", "Growth plates in young bones"]}, {"head": "Synovial", "points": ["Fluid-filled cavity inside a capsule", "Freely movable", "Hip, knee, shoulder, elbow, wrist", "Most joints used in asana"]}], "notes": "Here are the three classes side by side. Fibrous joints are stitched together by dense fibrous tissue, the same tough collagen tissue that makes up ligaments. Examples are the sutures between skull bones, the tooth held in its socket, and the lower ends of the tibia and fibula, which are bound by a strong ligament. Cartilaginous joints are joined by cartilage and allow a small amount of give. The discs between your vertebrae and the pubic symphysis at the front of the pelvis are the ones yoga teachers care about most. In children, the growth plates are also cartilaginous joints. Synovial joints have a real space between the bones, filled with fluid and wrapped in a capsule. Nearly every joint you name when you describe a pose, hip, knee, ankle, shoulder, elbow, wrist, is synovial."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "23", "label": "intervertebral discs in the adult spine", "body": "Each disc is a cartilaginous joint that allows only a little movement. Added together, those small movements give the spine its large range in Cat, Cow and twists.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "cobra", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": [], "caption": "Spinal extension shared across many small joints"}, "notes": "Here is a number worth remembering: twenty-three. There is no disc between the skull and the first vertebra, and none between the first and second neck vertebrae, and the sacrum and coccyx are fused, so the adult spine has twenty-three discs. Each disc is a cartilaginous joint with a tough fibrocartilage ring around a gel-like centre. On its own, one disc allows only a few degrees of bending. But the spine is a chain. When you add together small movements at many levels, you get the large curve of Cobra or the deep round of Cat. The spine also has small synovial facet joints at the back of each vertebra, which guide the direction of movement. So when a student backbends, you are watching many small joints share the work, not one hinge."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "The sacroiliac joint: a special case", "points": ["Where the sacrum meets each side of the pelvis", "Synovial in front, strong fibrous ligaments behind", "Movement is very small, a few degrees at most", "Built for load transfer, not for big range"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "triangle", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": ["pelvis"], "caption": "The sacroiliac joints carry load between spine and legs"}, "notes": "Yoga teachers hear a lot about the sacroiliac joint, often shortened to SI joint. It sits where the sacrum, the triangular bone at the base of the spine, meets the ilium on each side of the pelvis. It is a mixed joint: the front part is synovial, with a thin capsule, while the back is held by some of the strongest ligaments in the body. Its surfaces are rough and interlocking, and research consistently finds the movement to be very small, only a few degrees. Its main job is to pass the weight of the upper body into the legs. You cannot see SI joint movement in a pose, and you should be careful with claims that a student's SI joint is out. If a student reports pain in this area, adapt the pose and suggest they see a qualified health professional."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: three classes in your own body", "steps": ["Press gently on your skull: the fibrous sutures give no movement at all", "Slowly round and arch your spine: many discs each allow a little", "Lift one knee and circle the thigh: the synovial hip moves freely", "Notice how movement grows as you go from skull to spine to hip"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["trunk", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipR"], "caption": "Find each joint class standing in Mountain"}, "notes": "Let's make this real. Stand in Mountain. First, place your fingertips on your head and press gently. Underneath are the sutures, fibrous joints that knit tightly together as the skull matures; there is essentially nothing to move. Next, slowly round your spine forward and then lift your chest into a gentle arch. No single joint moves much, but you can feel the whole column change shape because many discs and facet joints each contribute a little. Finally, shift weight into your left foot, lift the right knee and draw slow circles with it. That is a synovial ball-and-socket joint, and the difference is obvious. Take a moment to notice the progression from no movement, to a little shared movement, to free movement. You have just felt the whole spectrum from the diagram."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Which joint is cartilaginous?", "options": ["A skull suture", "The pubic symphysis", "The hip joint"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The pubic symphysis joins the two pubic bones with a fibrocartilage disc and allows only slight movement.", "notes": "Take a moment before you answer. A skull suture is fibrous: bones knitted together by dense fibrous tissue with no movement in the adult. The hip joint is synovial: it has a fluid-filled cavity inside a capsule and moves freely. The pubic symphysis is the cartilaginous joint here. It sits at the front of the pelvis, where a pad of fibrocartilage joins the left and right pubic bones. It allows a small amount of movement, which increases in pregnancy when hormones soften the surrounding ligaments. That is one reason wide-legged poses and big asymmetrical steps can feel uncomfortable at the front of the pelvis during pregnancy. If you got this right, you can already sort joints by the tissue that holds them together, which is the whole point of this lesson."}], "guide": {"brief": "A joint, or articulation, is any place where two or more bones meet. Anatomists sort joints in two ways. The structural classification asks what tissue connects the bones. The functional classification asks how much the joint moves: immovable (synarthrosis), slightly movable (amphiarthrosis) or freely movable (diarthrosis). The two systems line up closely, which makes them easy to learn together.\n\nFibrous joints are joined by dense fibrous connective tissue and have no joint cavity. Examples are the sutures of the skull, the tooth in its socket (a gomphosis) and the lower tibia and fibula, bound by a ligament (a syndesmosis). They allow little or no movement. Cartilaginous joints are joined by cartilage and also lack a cavity. The intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis are made of fibrocartilage and allow a small amount of movement. The growth plates of young bones are temporary cartilaginous joints made of hyaline cartilage. Synovial joints have a fluid-filled cavity enclosed by a capsule, and they move freely. The hip, knee, ankle, shoulder, elbow and wrist are all synovial, which is why most of this module focuses on them.\n\nThe spine is a good example of how small movements add up. The adult spine has 23 intervertebral discs. Each disc, together with the small synovial facet joints behind it, allows only a few degrees of movement, yet shared across the whole column they produce the large curves of Cat, Cow, Cobra and twists. The sacroiliac joint is a mixed case: synovial at the front, bound by very strong ligaments behind, and moving only a few degrees. Its main job is load transfer between spine and legs, so be cautious with popular claims that it goes out of place, and refer students with pain in this area.\n\nThe key pattern to remember is a trade-off. As joints become more mobile, they rely less on their own structure for stability and more on the muscles around them.", "key_points": ["A joint (articulation) is any place where bones meet.", "Fibrous joints: dense fibrous tissue, little or no movement, e.g. skull sutures.", "Cartilaginous joints: cartilage link, slight movement, e.g. intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis.", "Synovial joints: fluid-filled capsule, free movement, e.g. hip, knee, shoulder.", "The spine's range comes from many small joints each contributing a little.", "More mobility usually means less built-in stability."], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain. Press gently on your skull and notice there is no give at the sutures. Slowly round and arch your spine and feel many small joints share the movement. Then lift one knee and circle the thigh, feeling how freely the synovial hip moves. Write down one pose that mainly uses the spine and one that mainly uses the hip.", "cue_it": ["Let the whole spine share the backbend, a little from every level rather than a lot from one place.", "Move from the hip joint itself, the crease at the top of the thigh, as you fold forward.", "If the front of the pelvis feels tender, bring your feet a little closer together."]}}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "The six synovial joint types and the movements each allows", "mins": 30, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Inside a synovial joint", "points": ["Articular cartilage caps bone ends: smooth and slippery", "Fibrous capsule seals the joint; synovial membrane lines it", "Synovial fluid lubricates and feeds the cartilage", "Ligaments link bone to bone and limit extremes"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-synovial", "caption": "Parts shared by every synovial joint"}, "notes": "Every synovial joint shares the same basic parts. The ends of the bones are covered with articular cartilage, a smooth, glassy layer of hyaline cartilage that lets surfaces slide with very little friction. Around the joint is a sleeve called the fibrous capsule, and its inner lining, the synovial membrane, makes synovial fluid. That fluid is thick like egg white. It lubricates the joint and also feeds the cartilage, which has no blood supply of its own. Cartilage gets its nutrients partly through loading and unloading, which is one good reason movement matters for joint health. Outside or within the capsule are ligaments, bands of dense connective tissue joining bone to bone. Some joints add extras, like the menisci in the knee or the labrum around the hip and shoulder sockets. We will meet those when we talk about stability."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Six shapes, six movement menus", "points": ["Hinge and pivot: one axis each", "Condyloid and saddle: two axes", "Ball-and-socket: three axes, including rotation", "Plane joints: small glides between flat surfaces"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-six-types", "caption": "The six synovial joint types with body examples"}, "notes": "Synovial joints are grouped by the shape of their surfaces, because shape decides which movements are possible. A hinge, like the elbow or the ankle, bends and straightens around one axis. A pivot rotates around one axis, like the first neck vertebra turning around the peg of the second, or the radius turning when you flip your palm. Condyloid joints, such as the wrist and the knuckles, move in two directions, forward and back and side to side, but cannot truly rotate. The saddle joint at the base of the thumb also moves in two axes and gives us opposition, touching thumb to fingertips. Plane joints are flat surfaces that glide a little, like the small bones of the wrist and the facet joints of the spine. The ball-and-socket joints, hip and shoulder, move around all three axes, including rotation."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Movement vocabulary by plane", "columns": [{"head": "Sagittal plane", "points": ["Flexion: angle closes", "Extension: angle opens", "Forward folds, backbends", "Ankle: dorsiflexion, plantarflexion"]}, {"head": "Frontal plane", "points": ["Abduction: away from midline", "Adduction: toward midline", "Side bends: lateral flexion", "Star shape, Gate pose"]}, {"head": "Transverse plane", "points": ["Internal rotation: turns inward", "External rotation: turns outward", "Spinal rotation in twists", "Forearm pronation, supination"]}], "notes": "To describe what a joint is doing you need a shared vocabulary. Movements happen in three planes. In the sagittal plane, which divides left from right, we have flexion, where the angle of a joint closes, and extension, where it opens. Forward folds are mostly flexion; backbends are mostly extension. At the ankle, lifting the toes toward the shin is dorsiflexion and pointing the foot is plantarflexion. In the frontal plane, which divides front from back, we have abduction, moving a limb away from the midline, adduction, bringing it back, and lateral flexion of the spine in side bends. In the transverse plane, which divides top from bottom, we have rotation: internal rotation turns a limb inward, external rotation turns it outward. The forearm has its own words: pronation turns the palm down, supination turns it up. Circumduction combines several of these into a circle."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Hinges in practice: elbow, knee and ankle", "points": ["Elbow hinge bends to about ninety degrees in Chaturanga", "The knee is a modified hinge: small rotation when bent", "Keep the knee tracking over the foot under load", "Ankle hinge: dorsiflexion as the shin travels forward"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chaturanga", "highlight": ["armR", "foreR"], "joints": ["elbowR", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Chaturanga: elbow flexion held by strong muscles"}, "notes": "Let's look at hinges in real poses. In Chaturanga, the elbows are hinge joints bending to roughly ninety degrees while the triceps and chest work hard to control the lowering. The knee is usually called a modified hinge. Mostly it bends and straightens, but when it is bent it can also rotate a little, and when it straightens fully the tibia rotates slightly to lock the joint. That small rotation is why we ask students to keep the knee tracking in line with the second or third toe in Warrior poses and lunges. Twisting a loaded, bent knee is a common way to strain it. The ankle joint, where the tibia and fibula meet the talus, is also a hinge. It dorsiflexes as the shin moves forward over the foot in Chair and Warrior I, and plantarflexes when you point your feet in Cobra."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Ball-and-socket: the hip in Warrior II", "points": ["Front hip: flexion with external rotation and abduction", "Back hip: abduction, near neutral or slightly extended", "Three axes allow many combinations at once", "The pelvis position changes what the hips must do"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Warrior II: two hips doing different jobs"}, "notes": "Ball-and-socket joints can combine movements in all three planes at once, and Warrior II is a great example. Look at the front leg. The hip is flexed because the thigh is forward of the pelvis, it is abducted because the leg is away from the midline, and it is externally rotated so the knee points over the toes. The back leg is quite different: that hip is closer to neutral or slightly extended, with some rotation depending on how the back foot is turned. The pelvis links the two hips. If a student turns the pelvis to face the long edge of the mat, the front hip must externally rotate more. If the pelvis turns a little toward the front, the demand drops. So hip comfort in Warrior II depends partly on pelvis position, which is why we let students adjust it rather than forcing a square or open pelvis."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: take each joint through its menu", "steps": ["Elbow: bend and straighten. Only one axis, so try to rotate it: nothing", "Forearm: turn palm up and down; the radius pivots over the ulna", "Wrist: move up, down and side to side; notice it cannot twist", "Thumb: touch each fingertip to feel the saddle joint's opposition", "Hip: standing in Tree, flex, abduct and rotate the lifted leg"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["thighL", "foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["hipL", "elbowL", "elbowR"], "caption": "Test each joint type standing near Tree"}, "notes": "Now let's feel each type. Start with your elbow: bend and straighten it. Then try to rotate at the elbow alone without moving the shoulder or forearm. You cannot, because a hinge has one axis. Now turn your palm up and down. That movement happens at pivot joints between the radius and ulna, not at the wrist. Next, move your wrist up, down and side to side. It is condyloid, so it moves in two directions but will not twist. Touch your thumb to each fingertip in turn. That is opposition, made possible by the saddle joint at the base of the thumb. Finally, stand near a wall, lift one leg toward Tree and take the thigh forward, out to the side and into outward rotation. The hip has all three axes. Notice how many more options it gives you."}, {"type": "check", "question": "A student turns their palms up in Savasana. Which joint type makes this possible?", "options": ["Hinge joints at the elbow", "Pivot joints between the radius and ulna", "Condyloid joint at the wrist"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Turning the palm up (supination) happens as the radius pivots around the ulna at the radioulnar joints.", "notes": "This question catches many people. It feels like the wrist is turning, but the wrist is condyloid and cannot rotate. The movement of turning the palm up is supination, and turning it down is pronation. Both happen because the radius, the forearm bone on the thumb side, rotates around the ulna at the radioulnar joints near the elbow and near the wrist. The joint near the elbow is a pivot joint. The elbow hinge between the humerus and ulna only bends and straightens. You will use this in class more than you might expect. In Downward-facing Dog, for example, students with elbows that hyperextend or wrists that ache sometimes benefit from a cue about the forearm, pressing the base of the index finger down, which involves the forearm bones and the muscles around them rather than the wrist alone."}], "guide": {"brief": "Synovial joints share a common design. The bone ends are capped with smooth articular (hyaline) cartilage. A fibrous capsule encloses the joint, and its inner synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint and nourishes the cartilage, since cartilage has no blood vessels of its own. Ligaments join bone to bone and limit extreme movement. Some joints add structures such as the knee's menisci or the labrum that deepens the hip and shoulder sockets.\n\nSynovial joints are named by the shape of their surfaces, and shape determines movement. Hinge joints (elbow, ankle, finger joints) move around one axis: flexion and extension. The knee is a modified hinge that can also rotate a little when bent. Pivot joints (the first two neck vertebrae, the radioulnar joints) rotate around one axis. Condyloid joints (wrist, knuckles) move in two axes but cannot truly rotate. The saddle joint at the base of the thumb moves in two axes and allows opposition. Plane joints (between the small wrist and foot bones, the spinal facets) allow small glides. Ball-and-socket joints (hip and shoulder) move around three axes, including rotation.\n\nTo read a pose you need the movement vocabulary. In the sagittal plane: flexion (the joint angle closes) and extension (it opens), plus dorsiflexion and plantarflexion at the ankle. In the frontal plane: abduction (away from the midline), adduction (toward it) and lateral flexion of the spine. In the transverse plane: internal and external rotation, spinal rotation, and pronation and supination of the forearm. Circumduction combines these into a circle.\n\nIn practice, most poses combine several actions. The front hip in Warrior II is flexed, abducted and externally rotated all at once. Because the pelvis links the two hips, changing pelvis position changes the demand on each hip, which is why flexible alignment choices serve students better than one fixed rule.", "key_points": ["Synovial joints share cartilage, capsule, synovial membrane, fluid and ligaments.", "Hinge and pivot: one axis. Condyloid and saddle: two axes. Ball-and-socket: three axes.", "Plane joints glide a little; the knee is a modified hinge.", "Flexion closes a joint angle, extension opens it; abduction moves away from the midline.", "Rotation happens in the transverse plane; the forearm pronates and supinates.", "Most poses combine several joint actions, and the pelvis links both hips."], "feel_it": "Sit or stand and take each joint type through its menu: bend and straighten the elbow, turn the palm up and down, move the wrist in two directions, touch the thumb to each fingertip, then lift one leg and move the hip in three planes. For each, say the joint type and the movement names aloud. Finish by naming the three actions of the front hip in Warrior II.", "cue_it": ["Let the front knee point in the same direction as your middle toes.", "Turn your palms up and feel the forearm bones rotate, not the wrist.", "Find the angle for your back foot that lets your front hip feel easy."]}}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Range of motion: tension versus compression and individual bone shape", "mins": 30, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "What stops the movement?", "points": ["Range of motion ends by tension or by compression", "Tension: tissue reaches its stretch limit, soft end feel", "Compression: something meets something, firm end feel", "Compression can be bone on bone or soft tissue"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-tension-compression", "caption": "Two ways a range of motion comes to an end"}, "notes": "Range of motion is how far a joint can move in a given direction. Every range has an end, and it helps to ask what is creating that end. There are two broad answers. The first is tension: a tissue on the opposite side of the movement reaches its stretch limit. That could be muscle, fascia, joint capsule or ligament. It usually has a soft, springy feel and is felt as a stretch. The second is compression: something runs into something else. That might be bone meeting bone, like the femoral neck touching the rim of the hip socket, or soft tissue meeting soft tissue, like the thigh pressing into the belly in a deep fold. Compression has a firm, blocking feel and is felt where the parts meet. This framework is simplified, but it is very useful for teaching."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Reading the end of a range", "columns": [{"head": "Tension", "points": ["Felt opposite the movement", "Soft, springy, stretchy sensation", "May change with regular practice", "e.g. back of thigh in a fold"]}, {"head": "Compression", "points": ["Felt where parts meet", "Firm, blocked or pinching sensation", "Bony limits will not change", "e.g. front of hip in deep fold"]}], "notes": "Here is a quick way to tell the two apart. Tension is felt on the side opposite the movement. In a forward fold, the hip flexes and the stretch is felt at the back of the thighs. It feels springy, and with regular practice many people gain range here, partly from tissue changes and largely from greater tolerance of the stretch sensation. Compression is felt where the parts come together. In a deep fold, a pinch at the front of the hip is a sign of compression. Bony compression will not change with practice, and pushing into it can irritate the joint. Soft tissue compression varies with body size and shape. The teaching point: when a student feels a stretch, they can explore. When they feel a pinch or a hard block, they have found an end, and the answer is a different angle or a prop, not more effort."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "About 125 degrees", "label": "average adult angle between femoral neck and shaft", "body": "Individual hips spread well either side of this average, and the neck also twists forward by differing amounts. Those differences change how far each hip can rotate and abduct.", "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-tension-compression", "caption": "Where the femoral neck meets the socket rim depends on bone shape"}, "notes": "Bone shape is one of the least discussed and most important facts in a yoga room. The femur, the thigh bone, has a neck that angles off the shaft toward the socket. In adults this angle averages about one hundred and twenty-five degrees, but real hips vary noticeably either side of that. The neck also twists forward by different amounts in different people, which anatomists call femoral version. The hip socket varies too: how deep it is and which way it faces. These differences change where the femoral neck meets the rim of the socket, and so where compression stops the movement. One student can rotate the thigh outward into Lotus easily while another, just as dedicated, stops early because bone meets bone. No amount of stretching changes the shape of adult bone. This is anatomy, not effort."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Same pose, different skeletons", "points": ["Two students cannot always reach the same shape", "Hip, shoulder and elbow shapes differ between people", "Judge a pose by its function, not its photo", "Offer options that suit the skeleton in front of you"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["thighL"], "joints": ["hipL"], "caption": "Tree: the lifted knee's angle depends partly on hip shape"}, "notes": "Put these ideas together and you reach one of the most freeing ideas in teaching: two students cannot always reach the same shape, however hard they work. In Tree, one student's lifted knee points straight out to the side while another's points forward at an angle. Both may be working at their true end range. Hip shape, the carrying angle of the elbow, the shape of the shoulder socket and many other differences change where each person's range ends. So rather than asking whether a pose looks like the photo, ask what the pose is for. Tree is for balance, attention and a stable standing leg. If the knee angle is different, the pose is still doing its job. Offer choices, such as the foot at the ankle or calf, and let students find their own version."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: tension or compression?", "steps": ["Lie on your back and hug one knee toward your chest", "Notice where you feel it: back of hip, or front of hip", "Take the knee slightly outward and hug again: does it change?", "Now come into Child's Pose with knees together, then knees wide", "Name each end as tension or compression"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "child", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR", "trunk"], "joints": ["hipR", "kneeR"], "caption": "Child's Pose: often a compression end at hips and knees"}, "notes": "Let's find both kinds of end in your own body. Lie on your back and slowly hug one knee toward your chest. Where do you feel the limit? Some people feel a stretch in the buttock or back of the thigh, which is tension. Others feel a pinch or fullness at the front of the hip crease, which is compression. Now take the knee slightly out toward the armpit on the same side and hug again. For many people the pinch eases, because the femoral neck is now angled away from the rim of the socket. Then come into Child's Pose with knees together. Notice whether the thighs press into the belly, a soft tissue compression. Now widen the knees and let the torso sink between them. Changing the angle changed what met what. That is the teaching insight in one small experiment."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Cue the sensation, not the shape", "cue": "If you feel a stretch, you can stay and breathe. If you feel a pinch or a hard stop, change the angle or add a prop.", "why": "A stretch usually signals tension, which may be explored gently. A pinch or block usually signals compression, which effort cannot change and may irritate.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "forwardfold", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipR", "kneeR"], "caption": "Seated Forward Fold: stretch behind, possible pinch in front"}, "notes": "Here is how this becomes a cue. Rather than telling students to fold further, teach them to read what they feel. A stretch sensation usually means tension, and they can stay, breathe and explore gently. A pinch, a sense of jamming, or a hard stop usually means compression. Pushing into that will not change the bone and may irritate the joint. So the cue gives a clear action for each: change the angle or add a prop. In Forward Fold, bending the knees or widening the feet often clears a pinch at the front of the hips. In Child's Pose, widening the knees or placing a blanket between thighs and belly does the same. This language also protects you as a teacher, because you are not guessing at a student's anatomy. You are helping them notice and respond to their own signals."}, {"type": "check", "question": "In a deep squat, a student feels a hard pinch at the front of the hip. What is the most likely limit?", "options": ["Tension in the hamstrings", "Compression at the front of the hip", "A weak core"], "answer": 1, "explain": "A pinch felt where parts meet, at the front of the hip in deep flexion, points to compression, which stretching will not change.", "notes": "Remember the rule of thumb: tension is felt opposite the movement, compression is felt where things meet. In a deep squat the hip is strongly flexed. The front of the hip is where the thigh and pelvis come together, so a pinch there suggests compression, either the femoral neck approaching the rim of the socket or soft tissues pressing together. Hamstring tension would be felt at the back of the thigh, and in a squat the knees are bent, which actually slackens the hamstrings. A weak core has nothing to do with this sensation. The teaching response is to change the angle. Widen the feet, turn the toes out a little more, or place a block under the seat so the hips do not have to flex as deeply. And if a pinch persists outside of class, suggest the student has it checked by a health professional."}], "guide": {"brief": "Range of motion (ROM) is how far a joint can move in a given direction. Every range has an end, and the most useful question a teacher can ask is: what is creating that end? A simple framework, popularised in yoga by teachers such as Paul Grilley and Bernie Clark, sorts ends into two kinds. Tension means a tissue on the opposite side of the movement has reached its stretch limit: muscle, fascia, capsule or ligament. It feels soft and springy and is sensed as a stretch away from the moving joint. Compression means something has met something else: bone on bone, or soft tissue on soft tissue. It feels firm and is sensed where the parts meet, often as a pinch.\n\nThe difference matters because the two respond differently to practice. Regular stretching often increases range limited by tension, partly through tissue adaptation and substantially through increased tolerance of the stretch sensation. Bony compression does not change with stretching, and pushing into it may irritate the joint. Soft tissue compression depends on body size and shape.\n\nIndividual bone shape is the key reason two students cannot always reach the same shape. The femoral neck meets the shaft at an average angle of about 125 degrees in adults, with wide variation either side. The neck also twists forward by differing amounts (femoral version), and hip sockets vary in depth and orientation. Together these decide where compression ends hip rotation and abduction. Similar variation exists at the shoulder, elbow and spine. No amount of effort reshapes adult bone.\n\nFor teaching, this shifts the goal from matching a photo to serving the purpose of the pose. Cue sensation: stay and breathe with a stretch; change the angle or add a prop with a pinch or hard stop. Offer options, and never press a student past a compression end.", "key_points": ["Range of motion ends by tension (stretch limit) or compression (parts meeting).", "Tension is felt opposite the movement; compression is felt where parts meet.", "Tension-limited range often improves with practice; bony compression does not.", "Bone shape, such as femoral neck angle and version, varies widely between people.", "Judge a pose by its purpose, not by how closely it matches a photo.", "A pinch or hard stop calls for a new angle or a prop, not more effort."], "feel_it": "Lie on your back and hug one knee to your chest, noting whether the limit is a stretch behind or a pinch in front. Move the knee toward the same-side armpit and notice whether the pinch eases. Then compare Child's Pose with knees together and knees wide. Label each end as tension or compression and note which change helped.", "cue_it": ["If you feel a stretch, stay and breathe. If you feel a pinch, change the angle.", "Let your knee point where your hip allows; the balance is the pose.", "Bend your knees as much as you need to fold from the hips without a pinch."]}}, {"id": "3.4", "title": "Joint stabilization: passive (ligament, capsule, bony fit) and active (muscle)", "mins": 30, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Two ways to hold a joint steady", "points": ["Stability keeps joint surfaces centred under load", "Passive: bony fit, capsule, ligaments, labrum, menisci", "Active: muscles, switched on and timed by nerves", "Every joint uses both, in different proportions"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m3-stability", "caption": "Hip relies more on passive stability, shoulder more on active"}, "notes": "Stability is a joint's ability to keep its surfaces well centred and to resist unwanted movement when load is applied. There are two kinds of support. Passive stabilizers work without any effort from you: the fit of the bones, the joint capsule, ligaments, and extras like the labrum, a rim of fibrocartilage that deepens the hip and shoulder sockets, and the menisci, C-shaped cartilage pads in the knee. Active stabilizers are muscles. They only help when they are switched on, and the nervous system decides when and how strongly, based on information from sensors in muscles, tendons and joints. Every joint uses both kinds, but in different proportions. Compare the hip and the shoulder in the diagram. The hip has a deep socket and thick ligaments. The shoulder trades that for range and depends much more on its muscles."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Passive and active stabilizers", "columns": [{"head": "Passive", "points": ["Bony fit: shape of the socket", "Capsule and ligaments: bone to bone", "Labrum and menisci deepen surfaces", "Always present, cannot be switched on"]}, {"head": "Active", "points": ["Muscles crossing the joint", "Tendons pull bones into position", "Nervous system controls timing", "Trainable through strength and control"]}], "notes": "Let's compare them in detail. Passive stability comes from structure. A deep socket holds a ball well. The capsule and ligaments act like straps that become taut near the end of a range. The labrum and menisci deepen or cushion joint surfaces. These structures are always present, but you cannot switch them on, and ligaments adapt slowly and do not spring back well once overstretched. Active stability comes from muscle. Muscles pull tendons, which pull bones into position, and the nervous system coordinates this in fractions of a second. The big difference for teachers is that active stability is trainable. Practice that builds strength, balance and control improves it. That is why so much of our cueing is about engagement, not to hold rigidly, but to have muscles share the load that would otherwise fall on passive structures at end range."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Hip versus shoulder: the mobility trade-off", "points": ["Hip: deep socket, strong ligaments, built to bear weight", "Shoulder: large head on a shallow socket, huge range", "Rotator cuff muscles keep the humeral head centred", "Arm balances ask the shoulder for strength and control"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "dog", "highlight": ["armL", "armR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Downward Dog: shoulders flexed and loaded, cuff working"}, "notes": "The hip and shoulder are both ball-and-socket joints, but they are built for different jobs. The hip carries body weight every time we stand or walk, so it has a deep socket, a labrum that deepens it further, and some of the strongest ligaments in the body. The shoulder is built for reach. The head of the humerus is large and the glenoid, its socket, is small and shallow; many anatomists compare it to a golf ball on a tee. That gives the shoulder the largest range of any joint, but it depends heavily on four rotator cuff muscles to keep the ball centred on the socket. In Downward Dog, Plank and arm balances we ask a joint designed for reaching to bear weight. It can do that well, but only if the surrounding muscles are strong and switched on. So progress weight-bearing on the hands gradually."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "The knee: ligaments, menisci and muscle", "points": ["Cruciate and collateral ligaments guide the knee", "Menisci deepen the flat tibia and spread load", "Quadriceps and hamstrings add active control", "Loaded twisting of a bent knee is the risk"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior1", "highlight": ["thighR", "shinR"], "joints": ["kneeR", "ankleR"], "caption": "Warrior I: front knee stabilized over the ankle"}, "notes": "The knee is a good example of both systems working together. The femur sits on a fairly flat tibia, so the bony fit alone is poor. Four main ligaments help: the anterior and posterior cruciate ligaments inside the joint limit the tibia sliding forward and back, and the medial and lateral collateral ligaments on the sides resist side-to-side bending. The menisci, two C-shaped pads of fibrocartilage, deepen the surface and spread load. Then the quadriceps and hamstrings, along with muscles of the hip and calf, add active control. In Warrior I and lunges, the front knee is bent and bearing weight. The situation to avoid is a loaded, bent knee twisting, where the foot stays fixed while the thigh rotates. Cue the knee to track toward the middle toes and let the leg muscles do their share of the work."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: switch on active stability", "steps": ["Stand on one leg in Tree and watch your foot and ankle muscles flicker", "Come to Plank; let your chest sink between your shoulder blades", "Now press the floor away and feel the shoulder muscles engage", "Notice the joint feels steadier when muscles share the load"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["footR", "shinR"], "joints": ["ankleR", "kneeR"], "caption": "Tree: constant small muscle corrections at the ankle"}, "notes": "Let's feel active stability at work. Stand in Tree and look down at your standing foot. You will see and feel small, constant adjustments in the foot and ankle. That is your nervous system reading information from sensors in the joints and muscles and firing muscles in response, many times a second. Nothing about your bones changed; your balance comes from active control. Now come into Plank. First, let your chest sink so the shoulder blades squeeze together and you hang on the joint. Notice how that feels. Then press the floor away, widen across the upper back and feel the muscles around the shoulder blades and shoulders switch on. Most people feel the joint become steadier and quieter. That shift from hanging on passive structures to supporting with muscle is what we are cueing for."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Micro-bend, then engage", "cue": "Soften the knees a touch, then firm the muscles of your legs as if you could hug the bones.", "why": "A slight bend keeps the knee off its passive end range and lets the quadriceps and hamstrings share the load.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR", "shinL", "shinR"], "joints": ["kneeL", "kneeR"], "caption": "Mountain: knees soft, legs active"}, "notes": "Here is a cue that puts active stability into practice. When students stand with straight legs, some push the knees all the way back into their end range. There the knee is resting on the back of its capsule and its ligaments, which is passive support. A tiny softening of the knee, not a visible bend, takes the joint off that end range. Then asking students to firm their leg muscles brings in active support. The image of hugging the bones is one that many students find helpful. You will use the same principle in the elbows in Plank and Downward Dog, and in the knees in Triangle and standing forward folds. It matters most for students whose joints move past straight, which is the topic of our next lesson. Keep the cue light: we want engagement, not gripping."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Why does the shoulder depend more on muscle for stability than the hip does?", "options": ["Its ligaments are the strongest in the body", "Its large head sits on a small, shallow socket", "It is a hinge joint"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The shallow glenoid gives little bony fit, so the rotator cuff must actively keep the humeral head centred.", "notes": "The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint, not a hinge, so the third option is out. And the strongest ligaments in this comparison belong to the hip, not the shoulder. The key is bony fit. The head of the humerus is large, and the glenoid socket on the shoulder blade is small and shallow. Even with its labrum, it covers only part of the ball. That design gives the shoulder its wonderful range but little passive stability. The four rotator cuff muscles, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis, wrap the front, top and back of the joint and continuously pull the humeral head into the socket. This is why strength and control work matter so much before loading the shoulders in Chaturanga, arm balances and headstands."}], "guide": {"brief": "Joint stability is the ability to keep joint surfaces centred and resist unwanted movement under load. It comes from two sources. Passive stabilizers work without effort: the bony fit of the joint surfaces, the fibrous capsule, the ligaments, and structures such as the labrum (a fibrocartilage rim deepening the hip and shoulder sockets) and the menisci (C-shaped fibrocartilage pads in the knee). Active stabilizers are the muscles that cross a joint, switched on and timed by the nervous system using information from sensors in muscles, tendons and joints.\n\nEvery joint uses both, in different proportions. The hip bears body weight, so it has a deep socket, a labrum, and very strong ligaments: it relies heavily on passive stability. The shoulder is built for reach. Its large humeral head sits on a small, shallow glenoid, so it has the greatest range of any joint but little bony fit. It relies on the four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis) to keep the head centred. Weight-bearing on the hands, as in Plank, Downward Dog and arm balances, asks a reaching joint to act like a supporting one, so it needs strength and gradual progression.\n\nThe knee sits between these extremes. The femur rests on a relatively flat tibia, so the bony fit is poor. The cruciate and collateral ligaments guide it, the menisci deepen and cushion it, and the quadriceps, hamstrings and hip muscles add active control. The main risk in yoga is a bent, loaded knee twisting while the foot stays fixed.\n\nPassive structures cannot be switched on, and overstretched ligaments do not readily return to their original length. Active stability, by contrast, is trainable through strength, balance and control. This is the anatomy behind cues such as soften the knees, press the floor away and engage the legs: they move load from passive end range onto muscle.", "key_points": ["Passive stability: bony fit, capsule, ligaments, labrum, menisci.", "Active stability: muscles, coordinated by the nervous system.", "The hip is mostly passively stable; the shoulder is mostly actively stable.", "The rotator cuff keeps the humeral head centred on the shallow glenoid.", "The knee combines ligaments, menisci and muscle; avoid loaded twisting of a bent knee.", "Active stability is trainable; overstretched ligaments do not readily recover."], "feel_it": "Stand in Tree and watch the constant small corrections of your standing foot and ankle. Then come into Plank, first letting the chest sink between the shoulder blades, then pressing the floor away. Notice how the shoulders feel in each version. Finish in Mountain, locking the knees back and then softening them with legs engaged, and compare.", "cue_it": ["Press the floor away and let the muscles around your shoulders switch on.", "Soften your knees a touch and firm the muscles of your legs.", "Keep your front knee tracking toward your middle toes as you bend it."]}}, {"id": "3.5", "title": "Hypermobility and hypomobility in the yoga room", "mins": 20, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "A spectrum, not a switch", "points": ["Hypermobile: joints move beyond the typical range", "Hypomobile: joints move less than the typical range", "Both are common and often cause no problems", "Yoga rooms tend to attract very flexible people"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "forwardfold", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR", "trunk"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "The same fold can be effortless for one student, out of reach for another"}, "notes": "Joint range sits on a spectrum. At one end are hypermobile joints, which move further than is typical. At the other are hypomobile joints, which move less. Most people are somewhere in between, and many have a mix, perhaps flexible shoulders and stiff hips. Hypermobility is common, especially in younger people and more often in women, and for many it causes no problems at all. It can even be an advantage in dance and yoga, which is one reason yoga classes tend to attract very flexible students. Hypomobility can come from bone shape, tissue stiffness, age, past injury or conditions such as arthritis. Neither end of the spectrum is good or bad in itself. What matters for a teacher is that each end needs a different emphasis: control and strength for the very mobile, patience and props for the stiff."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "9", "label": "points on the Beighton score", "body": "A screening tool clinicians use for generalised joint hypermobility: little fingers, thumbs, elbows, knees, and palms to floor. It is not a diagnosis, and teachers do not score students.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["elbowL", "elbowR", "kneeL", "kneeR"], "caption": "Elbows and knees are among the joints the Beighton score checks"}, "notes": "You may hear students mention the Beighton score. It is a nine-point screening tool that clinicians use to check for generalised joint hypermobility. One point is given for each side for bending the little finger back beyond ninety degrees, bringing the thumb to touch the forearm, elbow hyperextension beyond ten degrees and knee hyperextension beyond ten degrees. That makes eight. The ninth point is for placing the palms flat on the floor with the knees straight. Cut-offs depend on age. A high score shows generalised hypermobility, which on its own is not a disorder. Clinicians also recognise hypermobility spectrum disorders and hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, which involve more than flexibility, such as pain, fatigue or frequent joint instability, and are diagnosed through a full clinical assessment. Your role is not to score or label students. It is to teach well and refer."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "What you may notice, and what helps", "columns": [{"head": "Very mobile student", "points": ["Knees or elbows push past straight", "Sinks into end range, little muscle", "Helps: micro-bends, strength, slower pace", "Helps: work short of end range"]}, {"head": "Stiff student", "points": ["Hits end range early", "May strain or hold the breath", "Helps: blocks, straps, bent knees", "Helps: time and no comparison"]}], "notes": "Here is what these patterns can look like on the mat. A very mobile student may lock the knees back in standing poses or hyperextend the elbows in Plank and Downward Dog. They may drop into the deepest version of every pose and hang there on ligaments rather than muscle. Helpful responses include micro-bends at the knees and elbows, strength and balance work, a slower pace, and inviting them to stop before their end range and hold with muscle. A stiff student reaches end range early, may strain, and often holds their breath trying to match the room. Blocks, straps, bent knees, longer holds in comfortable positions, and a clear message that their version is complete all help. Notice that both lists are about adapting the practice. Neither requires you to know anyone's diagnosis."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Teaching the very mobile student", "cue": "Find your deepest point, then come back a little and hold the pose there with your muscles.", "why": "Working short of end range moves load from ligaments and capsule onto muscle, building the active control that very mobile joints rely on.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chaturanga", "highlight": ["armR", "foreR"], "joints": ["elbowR", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Chaturanga: strength matters more than depth"}, "notes": "This cue works well for everyone, but it is especially valuable for hypermobile students. When a very flexible student goes to their deepest point, they are often resting on passive structures, the capsule and ligaments, with muscles doing little. Over time that can leave joints feeling unstable or sore. Inviting them to back off slightly and then hold the pose with muscle shifts the load onto structures that can be trained. It also builds body awareness, which research suggests may be reduced in some hypermobile people. In Chaturanga, for example, going lower is not better. A student who holds a higher Chaturanga with steady elbows and shoulders is working the pose well. Say this to the whole room so no one is singled out, and praise control rather than depth."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: working short of end range", "steps": ["In Warrior I, bend the front knee as deep as you comfortably can", "Now rise a few centimetres until the legs feel strong", "Hold for five breaths, feeling muscles rather than joints", "Compare the two versions: which felt steadier and more active?"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior1", "highlight": ["thighR", "thighL"], "joints": ["kneeR", "hipR"], "caption": "Warrior I: hold slightly short of your deepest bend"}, "notes": "Let's try this in your own body. Come into Warrior I and bend the front knee as far as you comfortably can. Notice where you feel the pose. Now rise up a few centimetres, just until the front thigh and back leg feel strongly engaged. Hold there for five breaths and pay attention to what is working. Most people find the second version feels steadier and more muscular, and many find it more tiring, which is the point. Then try the same idea with your elbows in Downward Dog: if they tend to lock past straight, soften them slightly and press through the base of the index fingers. As you teach, you will see that working short of end range is not holding back. It is choosing strength and control over depth, which suits both flexible and stiff bodies."}, {"type": "check", "question": "A student says they are very flexible and asks you to test their Beighton score. What should you do?", "options": ["Score them so you can plan their practice", "Explain it is a clinical tool, offer control-focused options, suggest a health professional if they have symptoms", "Tell them flexibility means they cannot be injured"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Teachers do not screen or diagnose; they adapt the practice and refer when there are symptoms.", "notes": "The Beighton score is a clinical screening tool, and even in clinical hands it is only one part of an assessment. Scoring a student in class could create a label they carry away, and it is outside a yoga teacher's scope of practice. The right response is warm and practical. Thank them for telling you, explain that the score is something clinicians use, and offer ways to practise that build strength and control, like micro-bends and working short of end range. If they mention pain, fatigue, frequent joint slips or dislocations, suggest they see a doctor or physiotherapist. The third option is simply false: very flexible people are not protected from injury. Some have more joint instability, not less. This answer reflects the core rule of this course: teachers adapt and refer; they never diagnose."}], "guide": {"brief": "Joint range sits on a spectrum. Hypermobile joints move beyond the typical range; hypomobile joints move less. Many people have a mix, and neither end is good or bad in itself. Generalised joint hypermobility is common, more so in younger people and in women, and often causes no symptoms. Yoga classes tend to attract flexible people, so you will meet many. Hypomobility can come from bone shape, tissue stiffness, age, past injury or conditions such as arthritis.\n\nThe Beighton score is a nine-point screening tool that clinicians use to assess generalised joint hypermobility. It awards one point per side for bending the little finger back beyond 90 degrees, bringing the thumb to the forearm, elbow hyperextension beyond 10 degrees and knee hyperextension beyond 10 degrees, and one point for placing the palms flat on the floor with straight knees. Thresholds depend on age. A high score indicates hypermobility, not a disorder. Clinicians also recognise hypermobility spectrum disorders and hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, which involve features such as pain, fatigue or joint instability and are diagnosed through full clinical assessment. Yoga teachers do not score, screen or diagnose students.\n\nIn class, very mobile students may lock the knees or elbows past straight and rest in end range on their ligaments and capsule. They benefit from micro-bends, working short of end range, strength and balance work, and a slower pace. Stiff students reach end range early, may strain or hold the breath, and benefit from props, bent knees, time and a clear message that their version is complete.\n\nThe common thread is adapting the practice rather than labelling the person. Cue control over depth for the whole room. If a student reports ongoing pain, fatigue, frequent joint slips or dislocations, suggest they see a doctor or physiotherapist. Teachers adapt and refer; they never diagnose.", "key_points": ["Hypermobility and hypomobility are ends of a spectrum; many people have a mix.", "The Beighton score is a 9-point clinical screening tool, not a diagnosis.", "Hypermobility spectrum disorders and hEDS are diagnosed by clinicians, not teachers.", "Very mobile students need control, strength and work short of end range.", "Stiff students need props, time and freedom from comparison.", "Refer students with pain, fatigue or frequent joint instability."], "feel_it": "Come into Warrior I and bend the front knee as far as is comfortable, then rise a few centimetres until the legs feel strongly engaged. Hold for five breaths and compare. Repeat the idea in Downward Dog with the elbows softened slightly. Note which version felt steadier and which you would cue for a very flexible student.", "cue_it": ["Find your deepest point, then come back a little and hold it with your muscles.", "Keep a whisper of bend in your elbows and press through the base of your index fingers.", "Use a block to bring the floor closer; your version of the pose is complete."]}}, {"id": "3.6", "title": "Pose lab: reading every joint action in Sun Salutation A", "mins": 20, "slides": [{"type": "steps", "title": "How to read a pose", "steps": ["Pick one joint at a time, from ankles upward", "Name the plane: sagittal, frontal or transverse", "Name the action: flexion, extension, abduction, rotation", "Ask what limits it: tension or compression?", "Ask what stabilizes it: passive structures or muscle?"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": [], "joints": ["ankleL", "ankleR", "kneeL", "kneeR", "hipL", "hipR", "shoulderL", "shoulderR", "elbowL", "elbowR"], "caption": "Mountain: the neutral reference for every joint"}, "notes": "This final lesson pulls the whole module together. You will read Sun Salutation A joint by joint. Here is a simple method. First, choose one joint and work systematically, for example from ankles to knees to hips to spine to shoulders to elbows. Second, name the plane of movement. Third, name the action compared with Mountain, which we treat as the neutral starting point. Fourth, ask what limits the range: is the end created by tension or compression? And fifth, ask what holds the joint steady: passive structures, muscle, or both? You do not need to do all five steps for every joint in every pose. But practising this method a few times trains your eye, so that in a real class you can quickly see which joint is doing what and give a useful cue."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Arms up, then fold forward", "points": ["Arms up: shoulder flexion, scapulae rotate upward, elbows extend", "Forward fold: hip flexion with knees extended", "Spine flexes as the fold deepens", "Half lift: spine lengthens toward extension, hips stay flexed"], "visual": {"kind": "figure", "angles": {"view": "side", "trunk": 90, "neck": 90, "uL": 88, "fL": 90, "uR": 88, "fR": 90, "tL": -90, "sL": -90, "ftL": 0, "tR": -90, "sR": -90, "ftR": 0}, "highlight": ["armR", "foreR"], "joints": ["shoulderR", "elbowR"], "caption": "Upward Salute: full shoulder flexion"}, "notes": "The sequence begins in Mountain. As the arms sweep up into Upward Salute, the shoulders move into flexion, the arms travelling forward and up in the sagittal plane. The shoulder blades rotate upward to make room, and the elbows extend. Tight chest or back muscles can limit this by tension, and some students find their arms stay slightly forward of the ears; that is fine. Folding forward, the main action is hip flexion, with the knees extended. The limit here is usually tension in the hamstrings at the back of the thighs, and bending the knees reduces it. As the fold deepens the spine also flexes. In the half lift, the hips stay flexed while the spine moves back toward neutral, a gentle extension. Cue bending the knees generously here, so the lift comes from the hips and spine rather than the lower back rounding."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Plank to Chaturanga", "points": ["Plank: shoulders flexed about ninety degrees, elbows extended", "Lowering: elbows flex, shoulders move toward extension", "Ankles dorsiflex, toes extend on the floor", "Hips and spine held near neutral by muscle"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chaturanga", "highlight": ["armR", "foreR", "core"], "joints": ["shoulderR", "elbowR", "ankleR"], "caption": "Chaturanga: elbow flexion, shoulders moving toward extension"}, "notes": "Now step or jump back to Plank. The shoulders are flexed roughly ninety degrees relative to the trunk, the elbows are extended, and the ankles are dorsiflexed with the toes bent back against the floor. As the student lowers into Chaturanga, the elbows flex toward ninety degrees and the shoulders move toward extension as the upper arms come back alongside the ribs. This is a weight-bearing position for the shoulder, a joint that depends on active stability, so the rotator cuff and the muscles around the shoulder blades work hard. The hips and spine should stay close to neutral. That is not a joint action, it is stability, provided by the abdominal and hip muscles holding the body like a plank. Lowering the knees is a good option. It shortens the lever and lets the shoulders do the same actions with less load."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Cobra or Upward-facing Dog", "points": ["Spine extends; many small joints share the curve", "Hips extend with legs long behind", "Ankles plantarflex, tops of feet on the floor", "Elbows extend in Up Dog, stay bent in Cobra"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "cobra", "highlight": ["trunk", "armR"], "joints": ["hipR", "elbowR", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Cobra: spinal and hip extension"}, "notes": "From Chaturanga the student moves into Cobra or Upward-facing Dog. The main action is spinal extension. Remember the twenty-three discs and the facet joints: the curve should be shared along the spine rather than concentrated in the lower back. The hips are extended with the legs long behind, and the ankles are plantarflexed as the tops of the feet press into the floor. In Cobra the elbows stay bent and the back muscles do most of the lifting. In Up Dog the elbows extend and the arms bear more weight. What limits the backbend? Often tension at the front of the body and hips, and in some students compression at the back of the spine, where the bony parts of the vertebrae come close together. If a student feels a pinch in the low back, cue less height, not more effort."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Downward-facing Dog", "points": ["Shoulders fully flexed, arms in line with the ears", "Hips flexed, spine long and near neutral", "Knees extend as far as the hamstrings allow", "Ankles dorsiflex as heels reach toward the floor"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "dog", "highlight": ["armL", "armR", "thighR", "shinR"], "joints": ["shoulderR", "hipR", "kneeR", "ankleR"], "caption": "Downward Dog: flexion at shoulders and hips, dorsiflexion at ankles"}, "notes": "Downward-facing Dog combines several actions. The shoulders are in full flexion, the same action as Upward Salute but now bearing weight, so active stability matters again. The elbows are extended; for students whose elbows move past straight, a micro-bend keeps them off end range. The hips are flexed and the spine aims to be long. The knees extend as far as the hamstrings allow, and the ankles dorsiflex as the heels move toward the floor. Where are the limits? Tension in the hamstrings and calves is very common, and bending the knees lets the spine lengthen. Some students are limited at the shoulders instead. Heels do not need to touch the floor; ankle shape and calf length vary. From here the student steps forward, returns through half lift and forward fold, and rises through Upward Salute back to Mountain, repeating the actions in reverse."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: slow-motion Sun Salutation", "steps": ["Move through one round at half speed with a partner watching", "At each pose, pause and name three joint actions aloud", "Your partner checks: plane, action, limit", "Swap roles and compare what you each noticed"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "forwardfold", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR", "kneeL", "kneeR"], "caption": "Seated Forward Fold: the same hip and knee actions as the standing fold"}, "notes": "Now it is your turn. Pair up with another trainee. One of you moves through a single round of Sun Salutation A at about half speed. At each pose, pause and say three joint actions out loud. For example, in the forward fold: hips flexed, knees extended, spine flexed. Your partner listens and checks each one: which plane is it in, is the action named correctly, and what is limiting the range? Then swap roles. You may be surprised how much you notice when you slow down, like the moment the shoulders change from flexion to extension as you lower from Plank, or how the ankles go from dorsiflexed to plantarflexed as you roll over the toes. Write down anything you disagreed on and bring it back to the group. Reading joint actions quickly is a skill, and this kind of practice is how it grows."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Moving from Plank into Upward-facing Dog, what happens at the ankles?", "options": ["They move from dorsiflexion to plantarflexion", "They move from plantarflexion to dorsiflexion", "They stay in neutral"], "answer": 0, "explain": "In Plank the toes are tucked and the ankles dorsiflexed; in Up Dog the tops of the feet press down, so the ankles plantarflex.", "notes": "Picture the feet. In Plank the toes are tucked under, the heels reach back and the ankles are dorsiflexed, meaning the top of the foot and the shin are closer together than in standing. As the student rolls over the toes into Upward-facing Dog, the tops of the feet come to the floor and the ankles plantarflex, pointing the feet. Some students find rolling over the toes uncomfortable or impossible because of ankle or toe stiffness. The simple alternative is to flip the feet one at a time. This transition is a nice reminder that Sun Salutation A moves nearly every major joint through a range, which is part of why it works so well as a warm-up. When you can describe what every joint is doing in this sequence, you can read almost any vinyasa class."}], "guide": {"brief": "Reading a pose means describing it as a set of joint actions. A reliable method is to take one joint at a time, name the plane of movement, name the action compared with Mountain (the neutral reference), then ask what limits the range (tension or compression) and what stabilizes the joint (passive structures, muscle or both). Practise this on Sun Salutation A until it becomes quick.\n\nFrom Mountain, Upward Salute brings the shoulders into flexion, with upward rotation of the shoulder blades and extended elbows. Forward Fold is mainly hip flexion with extended knees, plus spinal flexion as the fold deepens; tension in the hamstrings is the usual limit, so bent knees are a sensible option. The half lift keeps the hips flexed while the spine lengthens back toward neutral.\n\nIn Plank the shoulders are flexed about 90 degrees relative to the trunk, the elbows extended and the ankles dorsiflexed with the toes extended on the floor. Lowering to Chaturanga flexes the elbows toward 90 degrees and moves the shoulders toward extension. The shoulder is weight-bearing and relies on active stability, while the abdominal and hip muscles hold the hips and spine near neutral. Knees down reduces the load.\n\nCobra and Upward-facing Dog are mainly spinal extension, shared across many small joints, with hip extension and ankle plantarflexion; the elbows stay bent in Cobra and extend in Up Dog. A pinch in the low back suggests compression and calls for less height. Downward-facing Dog combines full shoulder flexion under load, hip flexion, knee extension and ankle dorsiflexion. Hamstring and calf tension are common limits, and heels need not reach the floor. The sequence then reverses through half lift, fold and Upward Salute back to Mountain.\n\nSun Salutation A takes nearly every major joint through a range, which makes it a good warm-up and an ideal training ground for your eye.", "key_points": ["Read a pose joint by joint: plane, action, limit, stabilizer.", "Upward Salute: shoulder flexion, elbow extension.", "Forward Fold: hip flexion, knee extension, spinal flexion; hamstring tension limits it.", "Chaturanga: elbow flexion and shoulder extension under load; knees down reduces it.", "Cobra and Up Dog: spinal and hip extension, ankle plantarflexion.", "Downward Dog: shoulder and hip flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion."], "feel_it": "With a partner, move through one round of Sun Salutation A at half speed. At each pose, pause and name three joint actions aloud while your partner checks the plane, action and likely limit. Swap roles, then write down any action you disagreed about and look it up in this module.", "cue_it": ["Bend your knees as much as you need so the fold comes from your hips.", "Lower only as far as your elbows and shoulders stay steady, knees down is a strong choice.", "Share the backbend along your whole spine; if your low back pinches, come a little lower."]}}], "terms": [["Joint (articulation)", "Any place where two or more bones meet, such as the hip where the femur meets the pelvis in Warrior II."], ["Fibrous joint", "Bones joined by dense fibrous tissue with little or no movement, like the skull sutures that stay still in every headstand prep."], ["Cartilaginous joint", "Bones joined by cartilage that allows slight movement, like the intervertebral discs that each add a little to Cat and Cow."], ["Pubic symphysis", "The fibrocartilage joint at the front of the pelvis; it can feel tender in wide-legged poses, especially in pregnancy."], ["Synovial joint", "A freely movable joint with a fluid-filled cavity inside a capsule, such as the shoulder in Downward Dog."], ["Articular cartilage", "The smooth hyaline cartilage covering bone ends inside a synovial joint, letting the knee glide as you bend into Chair."], ["Synovial fluid", "Thick fluid inside a synovial joint that lubricates it and nourishes the cartilage; movement such as a gentle warm-up helps circulate it."], ["Joint capsule", "The fibrous sleeve enclosing a synovial joint; locking the knees back in Mountain rests the joint on the back of its capsule."], ["Ligament", "A band of dense connective tissue joining bone to bone, limiting extreme range, like the strong hip ligaments that tighten as you stand upright."], ["Hinge joint", "A synovial joint moving in one axis, flexion and extension, like the elbow bending in Chaturanga."], ["Pivot joint", "A synovial joint that rotates around one axis, like the neck turning to look up in Triangle."], ["Ball-and-socket joint", "A synovial joint moving in three axes including rotation, like the hip rotating outward in Tree."], ["Flexion and extension", "Flexion closes a joint angle and extension opens it; Forward Fold flexes the hips, Cobra extends the spine."], ["Abduction and adduction", "Abduction moves a limb away from the midline and adduction toward it; the front hip abducts in Warrior II."], ["Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion", "Dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot toward the shin, as in Downward Dog; plantarflexion points the foot, as in Cobra."], ["Range of motion", "How far a joint can move in a given direction, such as how far the thigh lifts toward the chest in a knee hug."], ["Tension", "An end of range created by tissue reaching its stretch limit, felt as a stretch opposite the movement, like the hamstrings in a fold."], ["Compression", "An end of range created by parts meeting, bone on bone or soft tissue on soft tissue, felt as a pinch, like the front of the hip in a deep squat."], ["Passive stability", "Support from structures that need no effort: bony fit, capsule, ligaments, labrum and menisci, like the deep hip socket in standing poses."], ["Active stability", "Support from muscles switched on by the nervous system, like the rotator cuff holding the shoulder steady in Plank."], ["Hypermobility", "Joint range beyond the typical; common and often harmless, but very mobile students benefit from micro-bends and control, for example in Downward Dog."], ["Beighton score", "A 9-point clinical screening tool for generalised joint hypermobility; it is not a diagnosis, and teachers do not score students."]], "misconceptions": [["With enough practice, anyone can reach the full expression of any pose.", "Bone shape varies between people and ends some ranges by compression. Stretching cannot reshape adult bone, so some shapes are simply not available to some bodies."], ["More flexibility always means healthier joints.", "Very mobile joints can rely heavily on ligaments and capsule at end range. They benefit from strength and control, and flexible students are not protected from injury."], ["A pinch in a pose means you need to stretch harder.", "A pinch usually signals compression, where parts meet. More effort will not change it; a different angle or a prop is the right response."], ["The sacroiliac joint regularly slips out of place and a teacher can put it back.", "The SI joint moves only a few degrees and is held by very strong ligaments. Pain here needs a qualified health professional, not a manual adjustment from a yoga teacher."], ["Locking the knees straight is the most stable way to stand.", "Locking rests the knee on its capsule and ligaments. A slight softening with active leg muscles shares the load with muscle."]], "flashcards": [["What is a joint?", "Any place where two or more bones meet, also called an articulation, such as the hip in Warrior II."], ["Name the three structural classes of joint.", "Fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial, ordered from least to most movable."], ["Give an example of a fibrous joint.", "The skull sutures, which do not move, even when you place weight on your head in a headstand prep."], ["Give two examples of cartilaginous joints.", "The intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis, which each allow a little movement in poses like Cat and wide-legged folds."], ["How many intervertebral discs are in the adult spine?", "23. Their small movements add together to create the large curves of Cobra and Cat."], ["What makes a joint synovial?", "A fluid-filled cavity inside a fibrous capsule, allowing free movement, like the shoulder in Downward Dog."], ["What does synovial fluid do?", "It lubricates the joint and nourishes the articular cartilage, which has no blood supply of its own."], ["What is articular cartilage?", "Smooth hyaline cartilage capping bone ends inside synovial joints, letting the knee glide as you sink into Chair."], ["What does a ligament connect?", "Bone to bone. Ligaments limit extreme range, such as the hip ligaments tightening as the hip extends."], ["Which synovial joint type moves in one axis of flexion and extension?", "The hinge joint, such as the elbow bending as you lower into Chaturanga."], ["Which joint type lets you turn your head to look up in Triangle?", "The pivot joint between the first two neck vertebrae, C1 turning around the peg of C2."], ["Which joint type is the wrist, and what can it not do?", "Condyloid: it moves in two axes but cannot truly rotate. Palm turning happens at the forearm."], ["What is special about the saddle joint?", "At the base of the thumb, it moves in two axes and allows opposition, used when you wrap the thumb around the big toe."], ["Which joints are ball-and-socket?", "The hip and the shoulder, which move in three axes including rotation, as the hip does in Tree."], ["Define flexion and extension.", "Flexion closes a joint angle and extension opens it: hips flex in Forward Fold, the spine extends in Cobra."], ["Define abduction.", "Moving a limb away from the midline, like the front thigh in Warrior II or the arms lifting out to the sides."], ["What happens at the ankles in Downward Dog?", "They dorsiflex, the shins moving toward the tops of the feet as the heels reach toward the floor."], ["What is tension as an end of range?", "Tissue reaching its stretch limit, felt as a stretch opposite the movement, like the hamstrings in Forward Fold."], ["What is compression as an end of range?", "Parts meeting, bone on bone or soft tissue on soft tissue, felt where they meet, like a pinch at the front of the hip in a squat."], ["Why can two students not always reach the same Tree?", "Hip bone shape, such as femoral neck angle and version, differs, changing where compression ends outward rotation."], ["What is the average adult femoral neck-shaft angle?", "About 125 degrees, with wide individual variation either side that changes hip range in poses like Lotus."], ["Name three passive stabilizers.", "Bony fit, ligaments and the joint capsule; the labrum and menisci also help, as in the deep hip socket during standing poses."], ["What is active stability?", "Support from muscles switched on by the nervous system, such as the ankle muscles constantly correcting in Tree."], ["Why is the shoulder less passively stable than the hip?", "Its large humeral head sits on a small, shallow glenoid, so the rotator cuff must keep it centred in poses like Plank."], ["What does the Beighton score measure?", "Generalised joint hypermobility on a 9-point clinical screen. It is not a diagnosis, and teachers do not score students."], ["What cue helps a very mobile student in Downward Dog?", "Soften the elbows slightly and press through the base of the index fingers, so muscles share the load instead of the joint's end range."], ["What happens at the elbows and shoulders as you lower from Plank to Chaturanga?", "The elbows flex toward 90 degrees and the shoulders move toward extension, with the rotator cuff working hard."]], "exercises": [{"type": "mcq", "q": "A student in Warrior II says their front knee will not point over the toes without their pelvis twisting. What is the most helpful response?", "options": ["Force the knee out with a hands-on adjustment", "Let them turn the pelvis slightly toward the front of the mat", "Tell them to stretch their hips every day until it works", "Have them straighten the front leg completely"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The pelvis links both hips; turning it slightly reduces the external rotation the front hip must find, which may be limited by bone shape."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Which structure makes synovial fluid?", "options": ["Articular cartilage", "The synovial membrane", "The ligaments", "The periosteum"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The synovial membrane lines the inside of the capsule and secretes synovial fluid."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a seated forward fold a student feels a strong stretch along the back of both thighs. What is limiting their range?", "options": ["Compression at the front of the hip", "Tension in the hamstrings", "A stiff sacroiliac joint", "The pubic symphysis"], "answer": 1, "explain": "A stretch felt opposite the movement, at the back of the thighs during hip flexion, is tension."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student in Plank has elbows that bend backward past straight. Which cue best supports the joint?", "options": ["Lock your elbows so the bones hold you", "Soften your elbows slightly and press the floor away", "Let your chest sink between your shoulder blades", "Bend your elbows to ninety degrees and hold"], "answer": 1, "explain": "A micro-bend takes the elbow off its passive end range, and pressing the floor away brings in active shoulder stability."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Why does the hip rely more on passive stability than the shoulder?", "options": ["The hip has a deep socket and very strong ligaments", "The hip has no muscles around it", "The hip is a hinge joint", "The hip has no joint capsule"], "answer": 0, "explain": "A deep acetabulum with a labrum and strong ligaments gives the hip much more bony fit than the shallow glenoid of the shoulder."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student tells you they have been diagnosed with hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. What is the best approach?", "options": ["Give them a Beighton test to confirm it", "Avoid all poses that move their joints", "Thank them, offer control-focused options and encourage them to follow their clinician's advice", "Tell them yoga will cure the condition"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Teachers do not test or diagnose; they adapt the practice, favour strength and control, and support the student's medical care."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "As a student rolls from Plank over the toes into Upward-facing Dog, what happens at the ankles?", "options": ["Dorsiflexion to plantarflexion", "Plantarflexion to dorsiflexion", "Inversion to eversion", "No change"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Tucked toes in Plank means dorsiflexed ankles; tops of the feet down in Up Dog means plantarflexed ankles."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Which statement about the sacroiliac joint is accurate?", "options": ["It is a freely movable ball-and-socket joint", "It moves only a few degrees and is held by very strong ligaments", "Teachers should adjust it back into place", "It is a fibrous joint with no synovial part"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The SI joint is synovial at the front and strongly bound by ligaments behind, with very small movement."}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each synovial joint type to its example", "pairs": [["Hinge", "Elbow"], ["Pivot", "C1 turning on C2"], ["Condyloid", "Wrist"], ["Saddle", "Base of the thumb"], ["Plane", "Between the small wrist bones"], ["Ball-and-socket", "Hip"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each part of a synovial joint to its job", "pairs": [["Articular cartilage", "Smooth surface covering the bone ends"], ["Synovial membrane", "Lines the capsule and makes the fluid"], ["Synovial fluid", "Lubricates and nourishes cartilage"], ["Fibrous capsule", "Sleeve that encloses the joint"], ["Ligament", "Joins bone to bone"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each pose moment to its main joint action", "pairs": [["Front hip in Warrior II", "Flexion, abduction, external rotation"], ["Elbows lowering into Chaturanga", "Flexion"], ["Spine in Cobra", "Extension"], ["Ankles in Downward Dog", "Dorsiflexion"], ["Shoulders in Upward Salute", "Flexion"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each joint into its structural class", "buckets": ["Fibrous", "Cartilaginous", "Synovial"], "items": [["Skull suture", "Fibrous"], ["Tooth in its socket", "Fibrous"], ["Lower tibia and fibula", "Fibrous"], ["Intervertebral disc", "Cartilaginous"], ["Pubic symphysis", "Cartilaginous"], ["Knee", "Synovial"], ["Shoulder", "Synovial"], ["Elbow", "Synovial"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each stabilizer as passive or active", "buckets": ["Passive", "Active"], "items": [["Hip ligaments", "Passive"], ["Shoulder labrum", "Passive"], ["Knee menisci", "Passive"], ["Depth of the hip socket", "Passive"], ["Rotator cuff muscles", "Active"], ["Quadriceps", "Active"], ["Ankle muscles correcting in Tree", "Active"], ["Abdominal muscles in Plank", "Active"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each sensation as more likely tension or compression", "buckets": ["Tension", "Compression"], "items": [["Stretch at the back of the thighs in a fold", "Tension"], ["Stretch across the chest with arms overhead", "Tension"], ["Pull in the calves in Downward Dog", "Tension"], ["Pinch at the front of the hip in a deep squat", "Compression"], ["Thighs pressing into belly in Child's Pose", "Compression"], ["Hard stop at the back of the low back in Cobra", "Compression"]]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Put the first half of Sun Salutation A in order", "items": ["Mountain", "Upward Salute", "Forward Fold", "Half lift", "Plank to Chaturanga", "Upward-facing Dog"]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Put the steps for reading a joint in a pose in order", "items": ["Choose one joint", "Name the plane of movement", "Name the action compared with Mountain", "Ask what limits the range", "Ask what stabilizes the joint"]}], "self_check": [["What is the difference between the structural and functional classifications of joints?", "Structural classification describes the tissue joining the bones: fibrous, cartilaginous or synovial. Functional classification describes how much the joint moves: immovable, slightly movable or freely movable."], ["Name the six synovial joint types and give one example of each.", "Hinge (elbow), pivot (C1 on C2), condyloid (wrist), saddle (base of thumb), plane (between carpal bones) and ball-and-socket (hip)."], ["Describe the joint actions of the front hip in Warrior II.", "The front hip is flexed, abducted and externally rotated at the same time. How much rotation it needs depends partly on how the pelvis is positioned."], ["How can a student tell whether their range is limited by tension or compression?", "Tension is felt as a stretch on the side opposite the movement, with a springy quality. Compression is felt where parts meet, often as a pinch or hard block."], ["Why might two dedicated students never reach the same shape in Lotus or Tree?", "Hip bone shape, including femoral neck angle, femoral version and socket depth and orientation, varies between people. These differences change where compression ends the range, and stretching cannot change adult bone."], ["Compare the stability of the hip and the shoulder.", "The hip has a deep socket, labrum and strong ligaments, so it relies mostly on passive stability. The shoulder's large head on a shallow glenoid relies mostly on active stability from the rotator cuff."], ["What is the Beighton score and what is the teacher's role regarding it?", "It is a 9-point clinical screening tool for generalised joint hypermobility. Teachers do not score or diagnose students; they adapt the practice toward control and strength and refer students with symptoms."], ["List the joint actions at the shoulders, hips, knees and ankles in Downward-facing Dog.", "Shoulders flexed, hips flexed, knees extended as far as the hamstrings allow, and ankles dorsiflexed."]]};
